# To-do lists, reminders and household tasks

Keep adding, completing, deleting and dismissing a reminder as separate actions. The person needs to know whether a change was saved and what is still outstanding. A to-do item is editable data that Home Assistant stores, not a step in a guided journey.

<h2 id="scope-and-native-ownership">What Home Assistant owns</h2>

Each to-do list is an entity. Its state is the number of incomplete items, and it can also be unavailable or unknown. Integrations differ in which item fields and operations they support. The native to-do cards save the items. These examples add no task store. They are simulated and do not show that a feature or adapter is installed. See the [Home Assistant to-do documentation](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/todo/), which opens outside this site.

This is reference specification HA-GAP-026. The application must also meet the integration acceptance checks below.

<h2 id="states-and-content-decisions">What to show for each condition</h2>

| Condition | Show | Do not |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Needs doing | The item text and the due date or time if one is supplied. | Invent a due time for an item that has only a date. |
| Completion requested | A pending state, kept apart from a saved completion. | Treat a ticked box as confirmation from Home Assistant. |
| Completed | A route to the completed-item actions that are supported. | Treat completing as deleting, or as dismissing every related reminder. |
| Save failed or unknown | The item, and what is known about the save. | Add the item again automatically when the connection returns. |
| Empty list or empty filter | Whether there are no tasks or no tasks match. | Say “All done” after a failed fetch. |

<h2 id="integration-contract">Rules for the integration</h2>

1. Read the item fields and operations the integration supports. Keep item identifiers, title, status and the type of due date. Do not assume every list accepts descriptions, repeats or due times.
2. Keep setting up a reminder apart from completing a list item. A saved reminder does not show that a notification was delivered or read.
3. Use the native item and storage path and the account's scope. While an add or update is pending, stop a second submission. Check the item Home Assistant returns before trying again.
4. Before a delete or a clear of completed items, name the count and the scope. Do not promise undo unless the implementation provides it.

<h2 id="accessible-interaction">Keyboard and screen reader use</h2>

- Give a completion control the full item name and its current status. Keep focus meaningful when a completed item moves to another section.
- Write due dates and overdue states as text, not only a coloured dot. Give the time zone for timed reminders.
- Keep long descriptions, notes and error messages readable at narrow widths. Do not put an interactive link inside another interactive row.

<h2 id="failure-and-recovery">When something goes wrong</h2>

- If a list becomes unavailable, keep only items that are clearly labelled as last known and stop saying the count is current.
- If an add has an unknown outcome, check the current list before repeating it. An identical title does not mean it is the same item.
- If a clear or delete only partly worked, list what remains and what failed. Do not say the list was cleared.

